\subsection{Phase C: anisotropic nonlinear basin and exact group geometry}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-c}

The nonlinear remainder in Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-block-basin} is already structured:
velocity, position, integral displacement, latent acceleration, and
accelerometer bias do not each generate their own quadratic self-term.  The
remaining conservatism comes from collapsing physically different coordinates
into one small Euclidean tube.  This phase retains the exact homogeneous LTV
couplings while assigning separate radii only to the quantities that enter the
nonlinear geometry.

Let the dimensionless active error be partitioned as
\begin{equation}
 \vct z=(\vct z_g,\vct z_\ell),
 \qquad
 \vct z_g=(\delta\vct\theta,\delta\vct b_g,
            \delta\vct a_w,\delta\vct b_a),
 \qquad
 \vct z_\ell=(\delta\vct v,\delta\vct p,\delta\vct S),
 \label{eq:widen-cylinder-split}
\end{equation}
with the held-bias coordinate omitted from $\vct z_g$ when it is frozen.  The
split is not a dynamical decoupling: all first-order cross-covariance and
Kalman-gain effects remain in $\widetilde A_k^{\rm cl}$.  It is used only to
bound the genuinely nonlinear remainder.

Define the direct $S$-to-attitude correction gain
\begin{equation}
 k_{\theta S}:=\sup_k\|\mat E_\theta\mat K_{S,k}\mat D_S^{-1}\|<\infty,
 \label{eq:widen-injection-gains}
\end{equation}
where $\mat D_S$ is the fixed dimensionless scale used in $\vct z$.  For the
accelerometer/magnetometer part of the same correction, define the compact-tube
incremental gain
\begin{equation}
 k_{\Delta g}:=
 \sup_k\sup_{\substack{\vct z_g\ne0\\
                        (\vct z_g,0)\in\mathcal T_k}}
 \frac{\|\Delta\vct\theta_{g,k}(\vct z_g)\|}{\|\vct z_g\|}<\infty,
 \label{eq:widen-S-peak-gain}
\end{equation}
where $\Delta\vct\theta_{g,k}$ contains the accelerometer and magnetometer
corrections but not the $S$ residual.  Finiteness follows from smoothness on the
compact accepted-measurement/geodesic family and
$\Delta\vct\theta_{g,k}(0)=0$.  Since the integral residual is exactly linear,
for every admitted state
\begin{equation}
 \|\Delta\vct\theta_k\|
 \le k_{\Delta g}\|\vct z_g\|+k_{\theta S}\|\vct z_\ell\|.
 \label{eq:widen-correction-split}
\end{equation}
This expression retains the actual full-gain $S$ cross-covariance path rather
than replacing it by a fictitious block-diagonal update.

On the anisotropic cylinder
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal C(r_g,r_\ell):=
 \{\|\vct z_g\|\le r_g,\ \|\vct z_\ell\|\le r_\ell\},
 \label{eq:widen-cylinder}
\end{equation}
all geometry-only quadratic products in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-nonlinear-load} are bounded by
$c_G r_g\|\vct z\|$ for a fixed scaled constant $c_G$.  Moreover,
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-correction-split} and Cauchy--Schwarz give
\begin{align}
 \frac{\|\Delta\vct\theta_k\|^2}{\|\vct z\|}
 &\le
 \sqrt{k_{\Delta g}^2+k_{\theta S}^2}
 \left(k_{\Delta g}r_g+k_{\theta S}r_\ell\right).
 \label{eq:widen-correction-ratio}
\end{align}
Hence a closed sufficient cylinder load is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 L_{\mathcal C}(r_g,r_\ell):=
 c_G r_g+c_5
 \sqrt{k_{\Delta g}^2+k_{\theta S}^2}
 \left(k_{\Delta g}r_g+k_{\theta S}r_\ell\right).}
 \label{eq:widen-cylinder-load}
\end{equation}
The sharper componentwise expression of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-nonlinear-load} may be
used by a certificate verifier; Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-cylinder-load} is a direct
analytical sufficient bound valid at every interior point of the cylinder.

\begin{theorem}[Anisotropic regional practical ISS]
\label{thm:widen-cylinder-iss}
Assume Phases A and B and choose $r_g,r_\ell>0$ such that the complete cylinder
and its admitted one-step prefixes remain inside the accepted measurement
geometry.  If
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{L_{\mathcal C}(r_g,r_\ell)<\ell_*}
 \label{eq:widen-cylinder-condition}
\end{equation}
with $\ell_*$ from Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-ell-star}, then every sufficiently small
Lyapunov sublevel contained in $\mathcal C(r_g,r_\ell)$ is robustly invariant
and satisfies the practical-ISS bound of Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-block-local-bound}.
The admissible linear-state radius is controlled by the actual cross-gain
$k_{\theta S}$ rather than by the attitude radius itself.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
All first-order coupling between the two blocks is already contained in the UES
transition.  On the cylinder, the geometry-only terms satisfy the first term of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-cylinder-load}, and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-correction-ratio} bounds the actual gain-weighted correction
term at every nonzero interior state.  Thus
$\|\widetilde\varphi_k(z)\|\le
L_{\mathcal C}(r_g,r_\ell)\|z\|$.
Equation~\eqref{eq:widen-cylinder-condition} implies the explicit Phase-B
smallness inequality, so the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local} applies
without replacing $r_g$ and $r_\ell$ by a common radius.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[What can and cannot be global in the linear coordinates]
\label{rem:widen-linear-global-limit}
If the attitude rows of all translational corrections were identically zero,
$k_{\theta S}=0$, the translational radius disappears from
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-cylinder-load}; the stable linear block can then be global
while the geometry block remains regional.  The deployed full-gain MEKF
generally has $k_{\theta S}\ne0$ because $S$ cross-covariance may nudge
attitude.  Hence the unchanged source admits a wider anisotropic cylinder, not
an honest global claim in $(v,p,S)$.  This explicitly identifies the
architectural obstruction to global translational certification.
\end{remark}

\subsubsection{Exact geometry of the implemented quaternion injection}

The source does not use the first-order quaternion $[1,\delta\theta/2]$ for
state injection.  It calls \texttt{quat\_from\_delta\_theta}, which uses the
trigonometric radial quaternion map outside a small series threshold and a
normalized fourth-order sine/cosine series inside that threshold.  Denote the
resulting unit quaternion by $q_c(\vct d)$ and its rotation matrix by
$\mathcal R(q_c)$.  For $\|\vct d\|\ge10^{-2}$,
\begin{equation}
 q_c(\vct d)=
 \left(\cos\frac{\|\vct d\|}{2},
 \frac{\sin(\|\vct d\|/2)}{\|\vct d\|}\vct d\right),
 \qquad
 \boxed{\mathcal R(q_c(\vct d))=\exp([\vct d]_\times).}
 \label{eq:widen-exact-injection}
\end{equation}
This identity is valid for arbitrary finite $\vct d$; the principal physical
rotation angle is, as usual, the geodesic representative in $[0,\pi]$ rather
than the unwrapped norm $\|\vct d\|$ when the latter exceeds $\pi$.
On the series branch, Taylor's theorem and normalization give a finite constant
$c_s$ such that the geodesic injection defect obeys
\begin{equation}
 d_{\SO(3)}\!\left(\mathcal R(q_c(\vct d)),
                    \exp([\vct d]_\times)\right)
 \le c_s\|\vct d\|^6,
 \qquad 0\le\|\vct d\|\le10^{-2}.
 \label{eq:widen-series-defect}
\end{equation}
The tiny branch defect may therefore be retained as a bounded deterministic
input rather than absorbed into an unspecified local smoothness constant.

The implemented covariance reset uses
\begin{equation}
 \mat G_r(\vct d)=\mat I+\frac12[\vct d]_\times.
 \label{eq:widen-reset-G}
\end{equation}
Because the skew part cancels in $\mat G_r^\top\mat G_r$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\mat G_r(\vct d)\|_2
 =\sqrt{1+\frac14\|\vct d\|^2},
 \qquad
 \det\mat G_r=1+\frac14\|\vct d\|^2>0.}
 \label{eq:widen-reset-G-bound}
\end{equation}
Thus the implemented covariance reset has no singularity at a correction angle
of $\pi$; its amplification grows algebraically with the correction magnitude.

For the physical attitude error
$\mat R_e=\widehat{\mat R}\mat R^\top$, define the exact group energy
\begin{equation}
 V_R(\mat R_e):=\frac12\operatorname{tr}(\mat I-\mat R_e)
 =1-\cos\theta_e.
 \label{eq:widen-SO3-energy}
\end{equation}
For every fixed $0<\theta_*<\pi$ and $0\le\theta_e\le\theta_*$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{1-\cos\theta_*}{\theta_*^2}\theta_e^2
 \le V_R\le\frac12\theta_e^2.
 \label{eq:widen-SO3-sector}
\end{equation}
Exact rotation identities also bound the vector-measurement linearization
remainder by $c_R(\theta_*)\theta_e^2$ on this entire compact geodesic chart.

\begin{theorem}[Group-geometric correction widening]
\label{thm:widen-group-correction}
In addition to the anisotropic theorem's prefix-safety hypothesis, require each
accepted correction to satisfy
\begin{align}
 \theta_e^-&\le\theta_*<\pi,\\
 \theta_e^+
 &:=d_{\SO(3)}\!\left(
   \exp([\Delta\vct\theta_k]_\times)\mat R_e^-,\mat I\right)
 \le\theta_*,\\
 \|\Delta\vct\theta_k\|&\le\delta_Q<\infty.
 \label{eq:widen-group-domain}
\end{align}
Include the series-branch defect of Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-series-defect} in the
bounded input.  Then the quaternion-injection and covariance-reset parts of the
nonlinear remainder have finite explicit bounds depending on
$(\theta_*,\delta_Q)$, with reset amplification bounded by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-G-bound}.  Consequently the anisotropic theorem
remains valid with a group-energy attitude coordinate on any exact
pre/post-correction chart strictly smaller than the antipodal set; no artificial
singularity at $\|\Delta\theta\|=\pi$ is required.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:widen-exact-injection} is the implemented radial quaternion
map; the small-angle branch differs from the exact exponential only by the
Taylor remainder in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-series-defect}.  Equation~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-G-bound}
follows by direct multiplication of Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-reset-G}.  The explicit
post-correction condition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-group-domain} prevents a large
finite injection from leaving the selected geodesic chart.  On that chart the
logarithmic attitude coordinate and $V_R^{1/2}$ are uniformly equivalent by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-SO3-sector}, and all exact rotated-vector residuals have
bounded second derivatives.  Hence the same nonlinear comparison proof applies
with constants depending on the compact geodesic chart and finite correction
budget rather than on a correction-angle cutoff below $\pi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Scope of the group result]
The theorem widens the geometry used to bound the implemented correction; it
does not claim global MEKF convergence.  The antipodal attitude topology,
measurement ambiguity, and gain-coupled translational injection remain real
limitations.  The useful conclusion is that the previous $\Delta_0<\pi$
restriction was a proof-device restriction, not a singularity of the source
reset map.
\end{remark}
